e Integration testing."],[3364,"`@osdk/unit-testing` reference"],[376,"`@osdk/vite-plugin-branch`, registered in `vite.config.ts`, for the Vite templates."],[1314,"`@osdk/vite-plugin-oac` lets you build an app against your ontology-as-code definitions before they exist in Foundry. On every change to `.ontology/`, it compiles the ontology again, regenerates an SDK into `.osdk/src`, and answers OSDK requests from the Vite dev server with data from a seed file."],[1893,"`@osdk/widget.client-react` connects a React tree to the Foundry host. `FoundryWidget` handles the message protocol, and `useFoundryWidgetContext` gives every component typed parameter values and an `emitEvent` function derived from your widget config."],[1895,"`@osdk/widget.client-react` supports React 18 and 19 and lists `@osdk/client` as a peer dependency. Add `@osdk/client`, `@osdk/react`, and your SDK package to call the Ontology."],[1819,"`@osdk/widget.vite-plugin` writes the manifest during `vite build` and runs developer mode during `vite`. Projects from `@osdk/create-widget` already include it."],[1246,"`<object>` is the object type's API name in lowercase with hyphens, so `Employee` gives `modify-object-employee`. Parameters skip vector properties, properties in `excludedProperties`, and properties set by `nonParameterMappings`; create and create-or-modify also skip derived properties. For interfaces, see Interface actions."],[6455,"`<td>` — Body cell"],[6453,"`<th>` — Header cell"],[6452,"`<thead>` — Table header row container"],[6454,"`<tr>` — Body row"],[4381,"`$and`, `$or`, and `$not` take clauses and nest to any depth:"],[4479,"`$as(Employee)` returns the concrete view of an interface object, with the object type's property names. `$as(Worker)` goes the other way:"],[4480,"`$as` makes no request. It only exposes the properties that were loaded:"],[4484,"`$as` throws when:"],[4632,"`$clone` builds a new local object. It does not write to Foundry; use an action for that. It throws if the changes include a different primary key:"],[314,"`$clone` throws if the update changes the primary key. It does not write anything to Foundry. Use it to build optimistic state for an action, as shown in Optimistic updates. To change data, apply an action."],[4362,"`$containsAnyTerm` and `$containsAllTerms` also take `{ term, fuzzySearch: true }`, which matches terms within a small edit distance, such as a typo:"],[2743,"`$count` is read as `data.$count`. A `\"property:metric\"` key is read as `data.property.metric`, so `\"salary:avg\"` becomes `data.salary.avg`. The result is fully typed from the keys you pass."],[4219,"`$duration` units:"],[4212,"`$groupBy` maps each property to a grouping. Several properties group by every combination of their values."],[4360,"`$in: []` matches every object, not none. When the array comes from user input, check for an empty array before you add the filter."],[3179,"`$in` with an empty array matches every object. If the list comes from user input, set `enabled: false` (see Fetch conditionally) or leave the filter out when the list is empty."],[4483,"`$includeAllBaseObjectProperties` cannot be combined with `$select`."],[4364,"`$interval` takes one rule. Rules nest through `$and` and `$or`:"],[4508,"`$link` sends no request until you call a load method. You do not need to `$select` anything for `$link` to work: the link resolves from the object's primary key."],[3266,"`$optimisticUpdate` runs synchronously when you call `applyAction`, before the request is sent. The `$` prefix keeps it apart from the action's own parameters, and it is not sent to the server."],[3181,"`$or` matches any of its clauses, and `$not` negates one clause. They nest:"],[4610,"`$orderBy: \"relevance\"` sorts by how well each object matches the text filters in your `where` clause, and adds a numeric `$score` to each object:"],[4608,"`$orderBy` maps property names to `\"asc\"` or `\"desc\"`. With several keys, the first key sorts first and later keys break ties:"],[3629,"`$orderBy` takes `{ [property]: \"asc\" | \"desc\" }`, or `\"relevance\"` after `nearestNeighbors`. `$includeRid: true` adds `$rid` to each object. `$includeAllBaseObjectProperties: true` loads every property of the underlying object type for interface sets."],[4179,"`$returnEdits: true` resolves to the edits the action made:"],[4382,"`$title` filters on the object type's title property and `$primaryKey` on its primary key, without naming them. Both are useful in code that works across object types:"],[4172,"`$validateOnly: true` runs the action's submission criteria and parameter constraints without applying it. It resolves to the validation result, valid or not, and does not throw on an invalid result:"],[1581,"`401 Unauthorized`"],[1582,"`403 Forbidden` or `PERMISSION_DENIED`"],[4737,"`ActionForm` does not add outer padding by default. Apply internal spacing by setting the form padding variables on the form or a wrapper element:"],[4681,"`ActionForm` fetches action metadata, renders fields for the action parameters, runs client-side validation, and calls the OSDK action when the user submits. The form title is hidden by default. Pass `showFormTitle={true}` to show it; the title uses `formTitle` when provided, otherwise the action display name, otherwise the action API name."],[4682,"`ActionForm` is a discriminated union: provide both `formState` and `onFormStateChange` for controlled mode, or omit both for uncontrolled mode."],[4712,"`ActionForm` is a lightweight OSDK component and does not yet match the full ActionForm in Foundry. The table below lists the main gaps to account for when adopting it."],[4736,"`ActionForm` is built on `BaseForm`, so the form layout and field styling use the same CSS variables documented in CSSVariables.md."],[1293,"`actionLevelValidation` is a list of rules that must all pass before a user can submit. `displayMetadata.failureMessage` is shown when a rule fails."],[2551,"`ActionTypesV2.list` returns one page of action types